# The 0061 and 0062 migration steps for .github/workflows/ci.yml
#
# WHY THIS IS A PATCH AND NOT A COMMIT: the GitHub App has NO workflow
# scope, and GitHub refuses the push outright:
#
#   "refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow
#    .github/workflows/ci.yml without `workflows` permission"
#
# This is the standing rule -- the owner applies workflow changes.
#
# APPLY IT WITH:
#
#   git apply docs/ci-ingredient-aliases-0061-0062.patch
#
# Generated against main at 4e10b10 (the merge of PR #261, the
# server-rendered dish page). The patch applies cleanly to that tree.
#
# WITH IT APPLIED
# ---------------
# .github/workflows/ci.yml applies the SAME 62 migrations as
# scripts/ci-migration-chain.sh and scripts/repro-ci-db.sh.
# scripts/validate-ci-migration-chain.py is what checks the three lists
# agree -- and it passes on the branch this patch was generated from:
#
#   CI migration chain OK (62 migrations, all three lists agree)
#
# WHAT IT ADDS
# ------------
#   0061_ingredient_aliases.sql
#     Creates public.ingredient_aliases and the first alias row: the
#     béchamel duplicate. `bechamel-sauce` keeps resolving because it has
#     a live public URL -- the alias redirects it to the canonical
#     ingredient instead of deleting the row and breaking the page.
#     Strictly additive: one new table, at most one alias row.
#
#   0062_ingredients_sourcing_vocabulary.sql
#     Adds the 21 reviewed ingredient rows (GUS-1108) -- the vocabulary the
#     sourcing availability rows attach to. Additive; publishes nothing.
#
# WHY 0061 AND 0062, AND NOT 0056 AND 0057
# ---------------------------------------
# PR #219 opened with 0056 and 0057. Main then merged 0058, 0059 and
# 0060, and ALL THREE are already applied to the LIVE database. An
# applied migration is frozen: its number cannot be reused, and a new
# migration that sorts BEFORE an applied one cannot be applied to live
# at all -- the chain runs in filename order.
#
# So both migrations were renumbered: 0056 -> 0061, 0057 -> 0062. They
# depend on nothing except `ingredients` and `ingredient_categories`, so
# running them at the end of the chain is safe.
#
# WHERE THEY RUN
# --------------
# After 0060, in all three lists. 0061 and 0062 are at the end of the
# migration sequence in ci.yml, scripts/ci-migration-chain.sh and
# scripts/repro-ci-db.sh, so the three agree.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS
# --------------
# The migration list lives in three files -- the CI workflow, the Nightly
# chain script and the local reproduction -- and all three must apply the
# SAME migrations. A migration applied in one and not the others is a
# schema that exists in one environment and not the others, and the route
# that reads it returns 500 where it is missing. That is exactly what
# happened to GET /api/dishes when categories.kind was in the live
# database and in no committed migration.
#
# This patch REPLACES two earlier ones:
#   docs/ci-ingredient-aliases-0056-0057.patch
#   docs/ci-ingredient-aliases-0056.patch
# Both name migration numbers that no longer exist.
#
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -1307,8 +1307,8 @@
       # `listed` is defined in terms of `family_class` and the
       # description.
       #
-      # It is numbered 0058 rather than 0056 because PR #219 already
-      # claims 0056 and 0057. Two migrations must never share a number.
+      # It is numbered 0058 rather than 0061 because PR #219 already
+      # claims 0061 and 0062. Two migrations must never share a number.
       #
       # It WRITES content rows (the backfill) and deletes one row, and
       # its proof runs inside its own transaction, so a failed proof
@@ -1337,6 +1337,34 @@
           PGPASSWORD=gustale psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -d gustale_ci -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
             -f db/migrations/0060_legacy_associations_published.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0060.log
           bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "the legacy associations, published as Unverified" "0060: ok" /tmp/m0060.log
+      # ── 0061: the ingredient aliases table ───────────────────────────
+      #
+      # Migration 0061 creates ingredient_aliases -- the mechanism
+      # behind the béchamel merge. A retired slug keeps resolving,
+      # because it has a live public URL.
+      #
+      # It is strictly additive: one new table, and at most one alias
+      # row (none at all on a database without the orphan).
+      #
+      # It was 0056 when this branch was opened. Renumbered to 0061 so
+      # it sorts after the migrations main has already applied to live
+      # (0058, 0059, 0060) -- an applied migration is frozen.
+      - name: Apply the ingredient aliases table
+        run: |
+          PGPASSWORD=gustale psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -d gustale_ci -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
+            -f db/migrations/0061_ingredient_aliases.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0061.log
+          bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "the ingredient aliases table" "INGREDIENT_ALIASES_OK" /tmp/m0061.log
+
+      # ── 0062: the ingredient sourcing vocabulary ───────────────────────
+      #
+      # Migration 0062 adds the 21 reviewed ingredient rows (GUS-1108) --
+      # the vocabulary the sourcing availability rows attach to. It is
+      # additive and publishes nothing.
+      - name: Apply the ingredient sourcing vocabulary
+        run: |
+          PGPASSWORD=gustale psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -d gustale_ci -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
+            -f db/migrations/0062_ingredients_sourcing_vocabulary.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0062.log
+          bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "the ingredient sourcing vocabulary" "0062: all 6 proofs passed" /tmp/m0062.log
 
       # The PER-DISH backfill assertions. LAST, because they assert the
       # state the whole chain produces: the reference dishes'
